            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For context, a mainstream whey isolate bought in a large tub on sale often lands somewhere around $1.20 to $1.70 per 25 g, and a big tub of whey concentrate blend can be cheaper still. So the Thorne premium is real money: drink one shake a day and you could pay roughly $25 to $35 a month more than with a budget isolate. Over a year, that is a few hundred dollars for the certification and the brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            That premium is easier to justify if you would otherwise be buying a different NSF-certified isolate, because certified products as a group cost more. Against other certified isolates, Thorne is competitive rather than cheap, and the difference between it and its closest rivals is usually a matter of cents per serving rather than dollars.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other two products in the table are where the value argument falls apart. Collagen Fit and RecoveryPro are not built to be efficient protein sources, and treating them as such makes them look very expensive. Judge them on what they are for, which we cover below, and buy your daily protein elsewhere in the range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Thorne vs. the Other Certified Isolates
          </h2>

          <p>
            Thorne's closest rival is <strong>Klean Athlete Klean Isolate</strong>, another practitioner-channel brand with NSF Certified for Sport status. Klean Isolate is unflavored and unsweetened, with about 20 g of protein per scoop, so it suits people who want to stir protein into coffee, oats or smoothies. Thorne is the better choice if you want a ready-flavored shake; Klean wins if you want no sweetener at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dymatize ISO100</strong> is the value play among certified isolates. Dymatize states it carries Informed Choice certification, it delivers about 25 g of protein per scoop, and it is usually considerably cheaper per 25 g than Thorne, with far more flavors. If you are not bound by an NSF-only rule, it is hard to argue Thorne is the better buy on numbers alone. Our <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize protein powder review</a> covers that range in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Transparent Labs takes a different route, publishing lab results rather than relying on a sport seal, and its grass-fed isolate carries more protein per scoop than Thorne. Momentous, which also leans on sport certification and sells into the same pro-team market, is the most direct lifestyle competitor; see our <a href="/momentous-protein-powder-review/">Momentous protein powder review</a> and our <a href="/transparent-labs-protein-powder-review/">Transparent Labs review</a> for those labels.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where Thorne pulls ahead is consistency across a whole range. If you also want certified creatine, electrolytes, a multivitamin and a plant protein for a dairy-free partner, Thorne can cover all of it under one certification. Where it falls behind is dose and price: most rivals give you more protein per scoop for the same money or less.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also the sweetener question. Thorne avoids sucralose, which some buyers prefer, but it does use a little cane sugar. If you want a stevia-only or completely unsweetened isolate, Transparent Labs or Klean Isolate respectively are the cleaner fits. If you are comparing a wider field of short-label powders, our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> guide ranks Thorne against them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rest-of-range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Plant Protein, Collagen Fit and RecoveryPro: Worth It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Thorne Plant Protein</strong> blends pea, brown rice and chia protein, and Thorne says the mix supplies all nine essential amino acids. A two-scoop serving delivers about 22 g of protein, with one chocolate panel showing 21 g, at around 130 calories for vanilla and 140 for chocolate, per retailer labels at the time of writing. It is NSF Certified for Sport and free of dairy, soy and gluten, which makes it one of the few certified vegan powders that is easy to find.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is the same as the whey: price. At roughly $49 for 20 two-scoop servings, Plant Protein costs a little more per 25 g than the whey isolate. Plant proteins also tend to carry more heavy metals than dairy, so the NSF contaminant screening is arguably worth more here than on the whey. If you are vegan and drug-tested, it is a strong pick; if not, compare it with the options in our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Collagen Fit</strong> is an unflavored bovine collagen peptide powder with 15 g of collagen per scoop, which retailer labels list as about 14 g of protein, plus 125 mg of nicotinamide riboside. It is NSF Certified for Sport. Collagen is an incomplete protein, missing tryptophan and low in leucine, so it does not replace a whey shake for muscle building. Buy it for its intended job, connective tissue support, and count it as a small top-up to your daily protein at most.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>RecoveryPro</strong> is a chocolate bedtime formula built on hydrolyzed whey concentrate with L-tryptophan, magnesium bisglycinate and GABA. Thorne's copy cites 13 g of whey protein per serving, and a tub holds 12 servings. That makes it a sleep-and-recovery product with some protein in it, not a protein powder you would buy for value. If you want protein before bed, a casein shake is far cheaper; see our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other items Thorne files under protein, such as the MediClear detox formulas and the Collagen Plus skin product, are aimed at different goals and are priced accordingly. They are outside the scope of this review, and none is a sensible pick if your goal is simply hitting a daily protein target.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Skip Thorne
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip Thorne if cost per gram is your main concern. Anyone bulking, feeding a family of lifters, or using two or three scoops a day will spend far less with a large tub of mainstream isolate or blend. The difference between roughly $2.50 and roughly $1.40 per 25 g adds up fast at that volume, and nothing in Thorne's label makes up for it if you do not need the certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you want a big single scoop. At about 21 g per scoop for the whey, you need more powder to hit 30 g or more per shake, which raises the effective cost again. Lifters who prefer one-scoop convenience at higher doses will be happier with an isolate that lists 25 g to 28 g per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if flavor variety is important. Two flavors of whey and two of plant protein is a narrow menu, and buyer reviews describe them as good rather than exciting. People who rely on dessert flavors to stay consistent with protein should look at brands like those we cover in our <a href="/pescience-protein-powder-review/">PEScience protein powder review</a> instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            And skip the whey if you are strict about zero sugar or have a milk allergy. The cane sugar is small, but it is there, and whey isolate still contains milk protein even though most lactose is filtered out. People with a diagnosed milk allergy, kidney disease or other medical conditions should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding any protein powder; nothing in this review is medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Where to Buy Thorne Without Getting a Fake
          </h2>

          <p>
            Thorne is popular enough to attract counterfeiters and lookalike sites. While researching this review we saw web domains using Thorne's name and product photos and listing Plant Protein at a fraction of the roughly $49 list price. Treat any Thorne product priced far below what Thorne, Amazon and established retailers charge as a red flag, because a fake certified product defeats the reason you bought it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The safe channels are Thorne's own site, Amazon listings sold by Thorne or shipped from Amazon's own inventory, practitioner platforms such as Fullscript, and established retailers. On Amazon, check the seller name on the listing before you buy, and avoid marketplace sellers offering unusual discounts. When the tub arrives, check that the seal is intact and the lot number and best-by date are printed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Travel packs are worth knowing about. Thorne sells single-serve packets of both whey flavors, which are useful for gym bags and trips, though they cost more per serving than the tub. If you are trying Thorne for the first time, a pack of single servings is a cheaper way to check you like the taste before committing to 30 scoops.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, buy the size you will finish. Whey isolate keeps well when sealed, but an opened tub that sits for months in a humid kitchen will clump and lose flavor. One 30-scoop tub a month for a single daily shake is the right rhythm for most people, and buying from a channel with reliable stock beats chasing a deal from a seller you do not recognize.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every Thorne product is a protein powder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thorne files collagen, detox formulas and a bedtime recovery drink under protein. Only the whey isolate and Plant Protein are efficient daily protein sources. Collagen Fit and RecoveryPro serve other goals, and counting them toward your protein target makes them look far more expensive than they are for their actual job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the NSF database check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Certification covers specific products and flavors, and listings can change. If you are drug-tested, search the exact product and flavor in NSF's certified products database before buying. It takes a minute, and it protects you if a listing changed, a flavor was reformulated, or a retailer is selling an older or uncertified version.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying from a too-cheap seller</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lookalike sites use Thorne's name and photos and list products well under the normal price. A counterfeit tub defeats the purpose of buying a certified product. Stick to Thorne, Amazon listings sold by Thorne or Amazon, practitioner platforms and established retailers, and check the seal and lot number on arrival.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the 21 g scoop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thorne's whey delivers about 21 g per scoop, not 25 g. If your meal target is 30 g or more, you need extra powder, which raises the real cost per serving. Compare products on cost per 25 g of protein rather than price per tub, or Thorne will look cheaper than it is.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating isolate as dairy-safe</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whey isolate is low in lactose, which helps many lactose-intolerant people, but it is still milk protein. Anyone with a diagnosed milk allergy should not use it, and should ask an allergist or dietitian about alternatives. Thorne Plant Protein is the dairy-free option in the range.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Thorne protein powder worth the money?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is worth it if you need NSF Certified for Sport status or want one of the most documented labels available. At roughly $2.40 to $2.70 per 25 g of protein at list price, it costs far more than mainstream isolates. Recreational lifters without a testing requirement can get the same protein quality for much less from a cheaper certified isolate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Thorne Whey Protein Isolate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  About 21 g of protein per scoop, from 25 g of whey protein isolate, in roughly 100 calories, per Thorne's label at the time of writing. Figures vary slightly between vanilla and chocolate. That is a smaller dose than many rival isolates, which often list 25 g or more per scoop, so heavier lifters may need more than one scoop.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Thorne whey NSF Certified for Sport?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Thorne lists its Whey Protein Isolate in both flavors as NSF Certified for Sport, along with its Plant Protein, Collagen Fit and RecoveryPro at the time of writing. Certification applies to specific products and flavors, so drug-tested athletes should confirm the exact item in NSF's certified products database before buying.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What sweetener does Thorne whey use?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Thorne sweetens its whey isolate with evaporated cane juice sugar and rebaudioside A, a purified stevia extract, rather than sucralose or acesulfame potassium. That leaves a few grams of sugar per scoop, roughly 2 to 3 g depending on the listing. For most people that is negligible, but strict zero-sugar dieters should know it is there.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Thorne protein good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can fit a calorie deficit well, because a scoop is about 100 calories for 21 g of protein and protein helps with fullness. It is not uniquely suited to weight loss, though; any lean isolate does the same job for less money. Our guide to the best protein powder for weight loss compares cheaper options with similar macros.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Thorne make a vegan protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Thorne Plant Protein blends pea, brown rice and chia protein for about 21 to 22 g of protein per two-scoop serving, per retailer labels, and it is NSF Certified for Sport. It is free of dairy, soy and gluten. It costs slightly more per 25 g of protein than Thorne's whey isolate at list price.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Where is the safest place to buy Thorne?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Buy from Thorne's own site, Amazon listings sold by Thorne or Amazon, practitioner platforms such as Fullscript, or established retailers. We found lookalike sites listing Thorne products far below normal prices, which is a classic counterfeit sign. Check the seller, the seal and the printed lot number, and avoid deals that look too good to be real.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
